WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.997
Here's a spring fixed to a wall, and a force pulling on its free end.

00:00:05.997 --> 00:00:08.083
How far does it stretch?

00:00:08.083 --> 00:00:11.560
You already know one way to answer that.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.319
Balance the forces.

00:00:13.319 --> 00:00:18.081
The spring pulls back with stiffness times stretch, K times q.

00:00:18.081 --> 00:00:20.385
The load pulls forward with F.

00:00:20.385 --> 00:00:23.380
At rest they're equal, so K q equals F.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.069
That's Newton's way.

00:00:25.069 --> 00:00:30.418
In this series you'll learn a second way, and it sounds completely different.

00:00:30.418 --> 00:00:37.920
Instead of balancing forces, we'll write down a single number, an energy, and look for the bottom of a bowl.

00:00:38.253 --> 00:00:40.322
Both ways give the same answer.

00:00:40.322 --> 00:00:50.133
But the energy way grows into something much bigger: the finite element method, the tool behind nearly every structural simulation you'll ever run.

00:00:50.433 --> 00:00:57.119
Here's the plan: springs first, then your first homework problem, then bars and finite elements, and your second homework problem.

00:00:57.119 --> 00:01:03.393
Along the way, the video will pause for you to try things in your practice guide, a short PDF that goes with these videos.

00:01:04.499 --> 00:01:05.483
Start with work.

00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:11.259
When a constant force pushes something through a distance, it does work: force times distance.

00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:15.499
On a graph of force against distance, that's the area of a rectangle.

00:01:15.832 --> 00:01:17.259
A spring is different.

00:01:17.259 --> 00:01:21.476
If you slam the full force on at once, the spring bounces around.

00:01:21.476 --> 00:01:27.832
We want the slow, settled stretch, so we pull gently, and the force grows as the spring stretches.

00:01:28.132 --> 00:01:31.015
At a stretch u, the force is K times u.

00:01:31.015 --> 00:01:37.890
It starts at zero and climbs in a straight line, up to its full value at the final stretch q.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.772
That full value is F, which equals K q.

00:01:41.066 --> 00:01:45.010
How much work does a force that keeps changing do?

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.928
Chop the stretch into tiny steps d u.

00:01:47.928 --> 00:01:55.026
Over one tiny step the force barely changes, so the work is force times d u: a thin strip.

00:01:55.332 --> 00:02:01.084
Add up all the strips, and you get the area under the force line.

00:02:01.084 --> 00:02:04.712
Here it's a triangle: base q, height K q.

00:02:04.712 --> 00:02:08.694
Half base times height: one half K q squared.

00:02:08.694 --> 00:02:12.852
That's the integral of K u d u, from zero to q.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:20.764
With numbers: K is twenty newtons per millimeter, and the final stretch is five millimeters.

00:02:20.764 --> 00:02:27.579
One half, times twenty, times five squared, is two hundred fifty newton millimeters.

00:02:29.333 --> 00:02:30.953
Where does that work go?

00:02:30.953 --> 00:02:35.340
The spring is now stretched, and it's holding that energy inside.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.053
Let it go, and it snaps back and gives the energy back.

00:02:39.353 --> 00:02:45.902
Energy stored in a deformed elastic body is called strain energy.

00:02:45.902 --> 00:02:48.320
We'll call it capital U.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:52.753
For a spring, U equals one half K q squared.

00:02:53.053 --> 00:02:54.804
Here's a picture to keep.

00:02:54.804 --> 00:02:55.996
Plot U against q.

00:02:55.996 --> 00:03:03.213
It's a parabola that opens upward: zero at q equals zero, and growing faster and faster as you stretch.

00:03:04.333 --> 00:03:06.866
Now the load's side of the story.

00:03:06.866 --> 00:03:12.316
Think of the load F as a heavy weight hanging on the end of the spring.

00:03:12.316 --> 00:03:17.613
It always weighs the same, F, no matter how far the spring stretches.

00:03:17.913 --> 00:03:27.488
When the end moves down by q, the weight drops by q, so it loses potential energy, F times q.

00:03:27.488 --> 00:03:33.254
That's called the work potential of the load, capital W.

00:03:33.254 --> 00:03:34.593
W equals F q.

00:03:38.229 --> 00:03:44.326
Pay close attention here, because this is the step everybody trips on.

00:03:44.326 --> 00:03:49.640
Strain energy has a one half, and the work potential doesn't.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:49.989
Why?

00:03:50.289 --> 00:03:57.273
The spring's energy builds up from nothing: its force starts at zero and grows, so its energy is a triangle.

00:03:57.273 --> 00:04:04.129
The weight is full size the whole way down: the energy it gives up is a full rectangle, height F, width q.

00:04:04.706 --> 00:04:10.178
In other words, U is the triangle, W is the whole rectangle.

00:04:10.178 --> 00:04:19.206
With our numbers, W is one hundred newtons times five millimeters, five hundred newton millimeters.

00:04:19.206 --> 00:04:20.666
Exactly twice U.

00:04:22.399 --> 00:04:23.832
Now combine them.

00:04:23.832 --> 00:04:27.625
As the spring stretches, it stores energy, U.

00:04:27.625 --> 00:04:30.491
And the weight gives energy up, W.

00:04:30.491 --> 00:04:36.559
So the total energy of the whole system, spring plus load, is U minus W.

00:04:36.859 --> 00:04:42.943
This is the definition everything else rests on, so let it land.

00:04:42.943 --> 00:04:50.358
The total potential energy, capital Pi, is strain energy minus work potential.

00:04:50.358 --> 00:04:52.259
Pi equals U minus W.

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:58.402
For our spring, that's one half K q squared, minus F q.

00:04:58.402 --> 00:05:06.112
With twenty and one hundred, Pi equals ten q squared minus one hundred q.

00:05:06.412 --> 00:05:08.448
Let's draw all three.

00:05:08.448 --> 00:05:10.871
U is the upward parabola.

00:05:10.871 --> 00:05:13.682
W is a straight line, rising.

00:05:13.682 --> 00:05:21.532
Subtract the line from the parabola, and you get Pi: a bowl that dips below zero.

00:05:21.866 --> 00:05:23.816
Look at a few values.

00:05:23.816 --> 00:05:26.509
At q equals zero, Pi is zero.

00:05:26.509 --> 00:05:29.481
At three, minus two hundred ten.

00:05:29.481 --> 00:05:32.916
At seven, also minus two hundred ten.

00:05:32.916 --> 00:05:34.866
At ten, back to zero.

00:05:35.199 --> 00:05:37.613
So where is the bottom of this bowl?

00:05:37.613 --> 00:05:39.959
Make a guess before we work it out.

00:05:44.599 --> 00:05:50.173
The bottom of a smooth bowl is where it's flat: the slope is zero.

00:05:50.173 --> 00:05:55.999
So we take the derivative of Pi with respect to q and set it to zero.

00:05:56.299 --> 00:06:00.121
Your turn: pause and do checkpoint one point one in your guide.

00:06:00.121 --> 00:06:01.819
Find the bottom of the bowl.

00:06:05.019 --> 00:06:08.321
The derivative of ten q squared is twenty q.

00:06:08.321 --> 00:06:12.748
The derivative of minus one hundred q is minus one hundred.

00:06:12.748 --> 00:06:15.899
So twenty q minus one hundred equals zero.

00:06:16.232 --> 00:06:20.338
Twenty q equals one hundred, so q is five millimeters.

00:06:20.338 --> 00:06:26.192
There's the bottom of the bowl, at five, where Pi is minus two hundred fifty.

00:06:26.526 --> 00:06:28.485
Now do it with letters.

00:06:28.485 --> 00:06:32.403
The derivative of one half K q squared is K q.

00:06:32.403 --> 00:06:34.703
The derivative of F q is F.

00:06:34.703 --> 00:06:37.002
So K q minus F equals zero.

00:06:37.002 --> 00:06:39.046
Which says K q equals F.

00:06:39.459 --> 00:06:47.381
This is the key step, so take it in slowly: setting the slope of the energy to zero gave us exactly Newton's force balance.

00:06:47.381 --> 00:06:50.859
Same equation, reached without drawing a single force.

00:06:52.833 --> 00:06:55.519
Let's say what just happened in general.

00:06:55.519 --> 00:07:01.565
Imagine trying every stretch the spring could possibly have: two millimeters, eight, five.

00:07:01.565 --> 00:07:03.513
Each one gives a value of Pi.

00:07:03.813 --> 00:07:15.998
Here's the principle: among all the geometrically possible displacements, the real one is the one that makes Pi stationary, and for a stable structure, a minimum.

00:07:15.998 --> 00:07:22.693
It's called the stationary principle, or the principle of minimum total potential energy.

00:07:22.993 --> 00:07:24.639
Why should we believe it?

00:07:24.639 --> 00:07:29.316
Because, as you just saw, its slope condition is exactly force balance.

00:07:29.316 --> 00:07:32.873
Minimizing Pi and balancing forces are the same thing.

00:07:33.206 --> 00:07:37.171
Geometrically possible means the trial must respect the supports.

00:07:37.171 --> 00:07:41.686
The end bolted to the wall never moves, so no trial is allowed to move it.

00:07:41.972 --> 00:07:45.165
Stationary means flat: the slope is zero.

00:07:45.165 --> 00:07:50.852
For a stable structure, the flat spot is the bottom of a bowl, a minimum.

00:07:51.732 --> 00:07:55.659
So, stepping back: one spring, two routes.

00:07:55.659 --> 00:08:00.147
Balance forces, or minimize Pi equals U minus W.

00:08:00.147 --> 00:08:06.692
Next, we'll see why the energy route wins when there are many springs.
